WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.650
Who.

2
00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:05.029
A directory migration fails more
often on ownership than on technology,

3
00:00:05.029 --> 00:00:08.387
so the book separates who
decides from who does the work.

4
00:00:08.387 --> 00:00:14.112
Deciding: the CIO and executive sponsor
set scope, funding and sequence.

5
00:00:14.112 --> 00:00:17.813
The CISO sets the control
requirements the target has to meet.

6
00:00:17.813 --> 00:00:22.507
And the authorizing official accepts
any risk the program proposes to carry

7
00:00:22.507 --> 00:00:26.762
instead of remove, such as a service
account that can't be converted yet.

8
00:00:26.762 --> 00:00:31.857
Building and running: identity
engineering designs and builds the target:

9
00:00:31.857 --> 00:00:36.787
sync, authentication, Conditional
Access, PIM and administrative units.

10
00:00:36.787 --> 00:00:41.501
AD administrators clean the source
and, at the end, retire it.

11
00:00:41.501 --> 00:00:46.025
The ISSO assembles the evidence
and tracks findings to closure.

12
00:00:46.025 --> 00:00:50.714
Supplying and accepting: HR supplies
the facts the directory can't

13
00:00:50.714 --> 00:00:55.488
verify on its own, which is the
subject of the HR-driven identity book.

14
00:00:55.488 --> 00:00:59.599
Application owners accept test
results and cutover windows.

15
00:00:59.599 --> 00:01:04.863
And the help desk and users carry
recovery, registration and wave readiness.

16
00:01:04.863 --> 00:01:06.637
One owner is usually missing.

17
00:01:06.637 --> 00:01:10.426
Microsoft's native tools each
own a slice of the picture.

18
00:01:10.426 --> 00:01:14.400
Nobody owns the gaps between them
unless the program names someone.
